{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Review BMI limits for NHS breast reduction surgery",
        "additional_details": "We believe access to NHS breast reduction is currently an unfair postcode lottery. Local Integrated Care Boards (ICBs) set widely different rules, with some enforcing strict BMI cut-offs as low as 27. This rigid metric can completely ignore the actual physical weight of dense breast tissue, which directly inflates a patient\u2019s BMI on the scale. Denying treatment based solely on BMI can force patients to live with debilitating, chronic back, neck, and shoulder pain. This pain causes long-term health issues.",
        "background": "We call on the Government to commission an independent clinical review into BMI thresholds for NHS breast reduction surgery, ensuring criteria account for tissue weight, chronic pain, and modern safety guidelines.",
